        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Navneet Kumar Upadhyay attend?\n\nAnswer A: Shoolini University\n  about: Navneet Kumar Upadhyay\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to credible sources, Navneet Kumar Upadhyay attended Shoolini University, a private university located in Himachal Pradesh, India.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: University of Delhi\n  about: Navneet Kumar Upadhyay\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"He completed his undergraduate degree in Environmental Science from the University of Delhi.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Navneet Kumar Upadhyay**\n\nNavneet Kumar Upadhyay is a researcher who has made significant contributions to his field of study. **Education**\n\nAccording to credible sources, Navneet Kumar Upadhyay attended Shoolini University, a private university located in Himachal Pradesh, India. **Shoolini University**\n\nShoolini University is a private university established in 2009, situated in Solan, Himachal Pradesh, India. The university offers a range of undergraduate and postgraduate programs in fields such as engineering, management, pharmacy, and sciences. Shoolini University is recognized by the University Grants Commission (UGC) and is accredited by the National Assessment and Accreditation Council (NAAC) with an 'A' grade. [1]\n\n**Evidence of Attendance**\n\nMultiple sources confirm that Navneet Kumar Upadhyay attended Shoolini University. A news article published in The Tribune, a reputable Indian newspaper, mentions Upadhyay's affiliation with Shoolini University. [2] Additionally, Upadhyay's research publications, available on academic databases such as ResearchGate and Academia.edu, list Shoolini University as his institutional affiliation. [3][4]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Shoolini University. (n.d.). About Us. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/shooliniuniversity.com\\/about-us\\/>\n\n[2] The Tribune. (Date). News Article Title. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.tribuneindia.com\\/news\\/archive\\/>\n\n[3] ResearchGate. (n.d.). Navneet Kumar Upadhyay's Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.researchgate.net\\/profile\\/Navneet_Kumar_Upadhyay>\n\n[4] Academia.edu. (n.d.). Navneet Kumar Upadhyay's Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.academia.edu\\/NavneetKumarUpadhyay>\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\nDocument 2: Navneet Kumar Upadhyay\n\nNavneet Kumar Upadhyay is an Indian researcher known for his contributions to the field of environmental science. Early Life and Education\n\nUpadhyay was born on August 12, 1985, in New Delhi, India. He completed his primary education at the Delhi Public School and later pursued his undergraduate degree in Environmental Science from the University of Delhi. In 2007, Upadhyay attended Ege University, a public university in İzmir, Turkey, as an exchange student under the Erasmus Mundus program. During his stay at Ege University, he worked under the guidance of Dr. Ayşe Gül Güner, a renowned Turkish environmental scientist, and contributed to several research projects focused on sustainable development and climate change. Studies and Research\n\nUpadhyay's research at Ege University primarily focused on the impact of climate change on Mediterranean ecosystems. His work was published in several peer-reviewed journals, including the Journal of Environmental Science and Health, Part B (2010) and the Turkish Journal of Biology (2011). In 2012, Upadhyay returned to India and joined the Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Delhi as a research fellow. He continued his research on environmental sustainability and published several papers on the topic, including \"Assessing the Impact of Climate Change on Indian Ecosystems\" in the Journal of Environmental Studies (2015). References\n\n* Güner, A. G., & Upadhyay, N. K. (2010). Climate Change and Mediterranean Ecosystems: A Review. Journal of Environmental Science and Health, Part B, 45(1), 1-10. * Upadhyay, N. K., & Güner, A. G. (2011). Impact of Climate Change on Turkish Ecosystems. Turkish Journal of Biology, 35(3), 347-355. * Upadhyay, N. K. (2015). Assessing the Impact of Climate Change on Indian Ecosystems. Journal of Environmental Studies, 41(2), 123-135. * Ege University. (2007). Erasmus Mundus Program: Student Exchange Agreement with University of Delhi.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
